== Imitation Learning, Distribution Shift, and Offline-to-Online Reinforcement Learning

Behavioral cloning (BC) reduces policy learning to supervised regression on expert demonstrations, but Ross and Bagnell @ross2010reduction showed that it suffers $O(T^2 epsilon)$ regret under covariate shift because errors compound as the policy drifts into states absent from the demonstration distribution. DAgger @ross2011dagger addresses this by iteratively querying the expert under the learner's induced distribution but requires repeated oracle access. De Haan et al. @dehaan2019causal demonstrated that BC is additionally susceptible to causal confusion, latching onto spurious correlations rather than the true causal features governing expert behavior. Laskey et al. @laskey2017dart proposed DART, injecting calibrated noise into demonstrations to force the learner to observe recovery behavior.

The transition from BC to on-policy RL fine-tuning introduces further failure modes. Wolczyk et al. @wolczyk2024forgetting showed that catastrophic forgetting is the primary mechanism behind poor BC-to-RL transfer, as RL gradient updates rapidly overwrite pretrained representations. Nikishin et al. @nikishin2022primacy identified primacy bias, whereby representations formed during early training resist subsequent adaptation. Rajeswaran et al. @rajeswaran2018dexterous proposed a practical mitigation, augmenting the RL objective with a decaying BC auxiliary loss to prevent catastrophic collapse during the transition.

Principled offline-to-online methods avoid this sharp transition. Nair et al. @nair2020awac introduced AWAC, using advantage-weighted regression to incorporate offline data into the online loop. Kostrikov et al. @kostrikov2022iql proposed IQL, which avoids out-of-distribution action evaluation through expectile regression. Ball et al. @ball2023calql introduced Cal-QL, calibrating pessimistic Q-values to prevent the performance dip during offline-to-online transitions. Uchendu et al. @uchendu2023jsrl proposed Jump-Start RL, where a pretrained guide policy controls early timesteps and progressively cedes authority to the RL policy. These methods collectively explain our observed BC-to-PPO collapse: compounding covariate shift pushes the policy off-distribution, on-policy optimization discards the stabilizing demonstration buffer, pretrained representations resist adaptation, and the value function must bootstrap from scratch. Off-policy algorithms with demonstration replay offer solutions but require departures from the on-policy PPO framework.
